Pure start/join LOGIC + started-only-reap check with in-process fake spawns:
no green threads and no heap allocation, so there are no roots to re-prove and
the collector has nothing to verify here. The end-to-end rooting behaviour of a
real worker pool is exercised by ../threadpoollinux and ../threadpooldarwin.

`BIT_GC=stress` does not report zero: 5/5 runs measured `collections=106`, 5
above stress.bit's documented scheduler-loop floor of ~101
(runtime/sched/workerrun.bit's `Worker.run`). The +5 traces to this program's
own two calls into `runtime/thread/threadpool.bit`'s `schedStart`/`schedJoin`,
both explicitly NOT `@nosplit` ("this runs on the boot/parent thread... may
allocate and reach safepoints" — their own header) and both containing a small
`while` loop over the 2-3 workers this program starts, so those loops do take
real back-edge safepoints. None of that touches a GC object: every array this
program passes them (`workerBlocks`/`schedBlock`/`argArea`/`doneWords`/
`handles`) is a fixed-size module-level array, not a `[]T` slice or heap
allocation, so nothing the collector does here can invalidate what the program
asserts on.
